export const OL1={title:'The event and the test plan',hook:'At every International Leadership Conference, voting delegates speak for their chartered associations—electing student leaders and voting on HOSA business. Organizational Leadership helps chartered associations choose those delegates and helps members master what a delegate needs to know: HOSA’s structure and history, its corporation and parliamentary procedure.',objectives:['Explain the dual purpose of Organizational Leadership.','Describe the test format and references.','Interpret the test plan and plan study time.','Explain how chartered associations select voting delegates.'],sections:[
['Purpose of the event',[
'Organizational Leadership is a **solo event** for the **secondary** and **postsecondary/collegiate** divisions with a **two-fold purpose**: **(1)** to help **chartered associations select their voting delegates** if another method does not already exist, and **(2)** to encourage members to **develop and apply knowledge of HOSA and parliamentary procedure** so they can represent their chartered association at ILC as official voting delegates.',
'It is described as a **chartered association event for voting delegates**—its main use is at the state (chartered association) level.',
'Proper business attire or the official HOSA uniform earns bonus points; bring photo ID and two #2 pencils.',
"Even members who are not selected benefit from preparing. The knowledge of HOSA and parliamentary procedure gained in this event helps in chapter meetings, officer roles and other leadership events.",
flip('Event facts',[['Type','Solo test event'],['Divisions','Secondary and postsecondary/collegiate'],['Purpose 1','Help select voting delegates'],['Purpose 2','Build knowledge of HOSA and parli pro'],['Test','50 multiple-choice items in 60 minutes'],['Tiebreaker','One essay question']])]],
['The test and references',[
'If a chartered association uses the HOSA Voting Delegate Test, it includes **50 multiple-choice items** and **one essay question**, with **60 minutes** to complete. New for 2026–27, the test has **50 questions**. In a **tie**, the **essay** is judged to break it.',
'References: the **HOSA Handbook, Sections A, B and C**; the **HOSA website**; **Robert’s Rules of Order Newly Revised In Brief, 3rd edition**; and the **First Fifty Years 1976–2026** HOSA publication.',
'Study the current editions—HOSA documents are updated regularly.',
"Read Handbook Sections A, B and C completely rather than skimming. Test items often come from specific sentences, lists and tables.",
chk('In a tie on the Voting Delegate Test, what breaks the tie?',['The essay question','A coin toss','The first test submitted','A speech'],'Essay is judged.')]],
['The test plan',[
'The test plan weights seven areas: **Voting Delegate Role and Responsibilities (24%)**, **Parliamentary Procedure (24%)**, **HOSA, Inc. (16%)**, **Organizational Structure of HOSA (12%)**, **History of HOSA (12%)**, **Positions and Responsibilities of the Executive Council (8%)** and **Membership (4%)**.',
'Nearly half the test covers the delegate role and parliamentary procedure, so give them the most study time.',
"Use practice questions in proportion to the test plan as well. If you write 50 practice items, about 12 should cover the delegate role and about 12 parliamentary procedure.",
ex('Planning study time','Divide 20 study hours by the test plan (plan invented for practice).',['Delegate role 24% → about 4.8 hours','Parliamentary procedure 24% → about 4.8 hours','HOSA, Inc. 16% → about 3.2 hours','Structure 12% and history 12% → about 2.4 hours each','Executive Council 8% → about 1.6 hours; membership 4% → about 0.8 hours'],'Time roughly matches the test plan (plan invented for practice).')]],
['Sample questions',[
'The guidelines include sample items: how many **regions** HOSA has; how a member **not in favor** of a motion votes (by saying **“no”**); and when **postpone indefinitely** can be used (**to dispose of an embarrassing main motion**).',
'These show the mix: HOSA facts from the Handbook, voting language from Robert’s Rules and the purposes of specific motions. Learn exact terms—RONR uses **“aye”** and **“no”**, not “nay.”',
'Look up the regions question in the current HOSA Handbook so you learn the exact answer.',
"Keep a list of terms where the exact wording matters—“aye” and “no,” “postpone indefinitely” and “postpone to a certain time.” Small wording differences often decide the correct answer.",
sort('Source to study for each question type',['HOSA Handbook or website','Robert’s Rules In Brief'],[['Number of HOSA regions',0],['How to vote against a motion',1],['Purpose of postpone indefinitely',1],['Executive Council positions',0],['Order of business',1],['HOSA’s founding year',0]])]],
['Selecting voting delegates',[
'At the chartered association level, voting delegate selection is at the **State Advisor’s discretion** or as outlined in the chartered association’s **bylaws and/or policies and procedures**. If no process is clearly outlined, the chartered association **may use the HOSA Voting Delegate Test**.',
'Members interested in serving should **check with their State Advisor** to learn their chartered association’s process.',
"Ask your State Advisor about deadlines, test dates and whether the test is given at a state conference or another time.",
"Some chartered associations combine the test with interviews, applications or officer recommendations. Knowing the full process helps you prepare for every step.",
order('Becoming a voting delegate',['Learn your chartered association’s process','Talk with your State Advisor','Study the references','Take the test if it is used','Be selected and notified','Prepare for ILC duties'])]],
['How many delegates?',[
'The number of voting delegates depends on a chartered association’s **membership**. From the guidelines’ table: **0–25 members: 0 delegates**; **25–100: 1**; **101–1,000: 2**; **1,001–3,000: 3**; **3,001–5,000: 4**; and **one more delegate for each additional 2,000 members**, up to **24 delegates** for **43,001–45,000** members.',
'Understanding the table helps you answer membership and delegate questions on the test.',
"Because the ranges in the delegate table overlap at 25 members in the printed table, check the current guidelines and Handbook if a question uses a boundary number.",
ex('Reading the delegate table','A chartered association has 6,200 members. How many voting delegates? (Practice item.)',['Find the range: 5,001–7,000','The table lists 5 delegates for that range','Check: 3,001–5,000 = 4, so the next range adds 1'],'5 voting delegates.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Delegate questions',{a:{text:'A chartered association has 850 members. How many voting delegates does it receive? (Practice item.)',choices:[['2','b','Correct—101–1,000 members.',true],['1','b','One delegate is for 25–100 members.',false]]},b:{text:'A member votes against a motion during a voice vote. What does the member say?',choices:[['“No”','c','Correct—RONR uses aye and no.',true],['“Nay”','c','The sample item’s answer is “no.”',false]]},c:{text:'You add both facts to your study cards.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Organizational Leadership (solo; secondary and postsecondary) helps chartered associations select voting delegates and builds knowledge of HOSA and parliamentary procedure.','The optional Voting Delegate Test has 50 multiple-choice items and one essay (tiebreaker) in 60 minutes, based on the HOSA Handbook Sections A–C, the HOSA website, RONR In Brief (3rd ed.) and First Fifty Years.','Test plan: delegate role 24%, parliamentary procedure 24%, HOSA, Inc. 16%, structure 12%, history 12%, Executive Council 8%, membership 4%; delegate numbers follow membership (e.g., 101–1,000 = 2; +1 per 2,000 above 3,000).'],terms:[['Voting delegate','A member who officially votes for a chartered association at ILC.'],['Chartered association','A state or other unit of HOSA chartered by the international organization.'],['Test plan','The weighting of topics on a test.']],mcqs:[q('How many multiple-choice items are on the test?','50','100','25','75','New for 2026–27.'),q('What breaks a tie?','The essay question','A speech','The judges’ vote','Time of submission','Rule.'),q('Which two areas are each 24% of the test?','Delegate role and parliamentary procedure','History and membership','HOSA, Inc. and structure','Executive Council and history','Test plan.'),q('How does a member vote against a motion by voice?','“No”','“Nay”','“Negative”','By staying silent','Sample item.'),q('When can postpone indefinitely be used?','To dispose of an embarrassing main motion','To delay to the next meeting','To make a motion germane','To recess','Sample item.'),q('How many delegates for 101–1,000 members?','2','1','3','0','Table.'),q('Who decides the delegate selection process at the chartered association level?','The State Advisor or chartered association bylaws and policies','The judges','Any member','ILC staff only','Rule.'),q('How many delegates for a chartered association with 4,000 members?','4','2','3','6','Table.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the purpose of Organizational Leadership, its test and how voting delegates are selected and allocated.',rubric:[['Purpose',['voting delegates','chartered association','knowledge','parliamentary procedure','represent']],['Test',['50','essay','60 minutes','tie','handbook','robert’s rules','first fifty years']],['Test plan',['24%','16%','12%','8%','4%','delegate role']],['Selection and allocation',['state advisor','bylaws','policies','membership','table','delegates']]],model:'Organizational Leadership is a solo event for secondary and postsecondary members with two purposes: to help chartered associations select their voting delegates when no other method exists and to encourage members to learn about HOSA and parliamentary procedure so they can represent their chartered association at ILC. At the chartered association level, delegate selection is up to the State Advisor or the association’s bylaws and policies; if no process exists, the association may use the HOSA Voting Delegate Test, which has 50 multiple-choice items and one essay question in 60 minutes, with the essay judged to break ties. The test draws from the HOSA Handbook Sections A, B and C, the HOSA website, Robert’s Rules of Order Newly Revised In Brief and the First Fifty Years 1976–2026 publication. Its test plan gives 24% each to the voting delegate role and parliamentary procedure, 16% to HOSA, Inc., 12% each to organizational structure and history, 8% to Executive Council positions and 4% to membership, so I would spend about half my study time on the first two areas. The number of delegates depends on membership: a chartered association with 25 to 100 members receives one delegate, 101 to 1,000 receives two, 1,001 to 3,000 receives three, 3,001 to 5,000 receives four, and one more is added for each additional 2,000 members, up to 24. Anyone interested should ask their State Advisor about the process.'},sources:[RULES,HOSA]};

export const OL2={title:'The voting delegate role and responsibilities',hook:'Being a voting delegate is a serious responsibility: you represent every member of your chartered association when HOSA makes decisions. This area is 24% of the test. Delegates prepare before ILC, attend required sessions at the conference and vote thoughtfully on behalf of their members.',objectives:['Describe a voting delegate’s responsibilities before ILC.','List the sessions delegates attend at ILC.','Explain how delegates represent their chartered association.','Describe how delegates are recognized.'],sections:[
['Before ILC',[
'Each chartered association notifies its selected voting delegates. Before ILC, delegates must review: **(a)** the **Voting Delegate Packet**, which State Advisors receive from HOSA Headquarters **60 days before ILC** and share with their delegates, and **(b)** the **Executive Council applicants’ resumes** and **YouTube videos** posted on the HOSA website.',
'Careful preparation lets delegates make informed decisions rather than voting on first impressions at the conference.',
'Take notes on each candidate and each item of business in the packet.',
"Watching candidates’ videos more than once helps you notice details about their goals and communication that you might miss the first time.",
"Organize the packet’s contents into a short summary page listing each item of business, the decision required and your initial thoughts.",
"Discuss the packet with your State Advisor and fellow delegates before ILC. Shared understanding helps your delegation prepare questions and plan for the caucus.",
order('Delegate preparation before ILC',['Receive notification of selection','Receive the Voting Delegate Packet from the State Advisor','Read the packet carefully','Read Executive Council applicants’ resumes','Watch the candidates’ videos','Take notes and prepare questions'])]],
['At ILC',[
'At ILC, voting delegates must attend and participate in: **Voting Delegate Orientation**, the **Meet the Candidates Breakfast**, the **Chartered Association Caucus** (if desired), the **Business Session** and the **Recognition Session**. Check the ILC schedule for exact dates and times.',
'Orientation explains procedures and expectations. The breakfast gives delegates a chance to meet candidates. The caucus lets a chartered association’s delegates discuss decisions together. The business session is where official votes occur.',
"Bring a notebook to orientation and the breakfast. Notes help you remember procedures and candidates’ answers during the business session.",
"Arrive early to each required session. Being on time shows respect for the process and ensures you receive all instructions.",
match('ILC session and purpose',[['Voting Delegate Orientation','Learn procedures and expectations'],['Meet the Candidates Breakfast','Meet Executive Council candidates'],['Chartered Association Caucus','Discuss decisions as a delegation'],['Business Session','Conduct official business and vote'],['Recognition Session','Recognize delegates and achievements'],['ILC schedule','Exact dates and times']])]],
['Representing your members',[
'A delegate votes on behalf of the **entire chartered association**, not just personal preferences. Good delegates listen to members and advisors, study the issues, evaluate candidates fairly and vote for what best serves HOSA and their members.',
'Delegates should act with **integrity**: attend all required sessions, stay informed, follow rules, respect confidentiality where required and conduct themselves professionally as representatives of their chartered association.',
"Delegates should also respect the views of other chartered associations. Healthy debate and respectful disagreement strengthen the organization.",
"Delegates should avoid making promises to candidates or other delegates before reviewing all information. Keeping an open mind until the vote ensures a fair decision.",
"Remember that delegates are trusted with information that may be confidential until released. Follow instructions about what can be shared and when.",
sort('Responsible delegate behavior?',['Responsible','Irresponsible'],[['Reading the Voting Delegate Packet before ILC',0],['Skipping the Business Session',1],['Evaluating candidates using resumes and videos',0],['Voting for a friend without reviewing qualifications',1],['Discussing issues at the caucus',0],['Ignoring orientation instructions',1]])]],
['Recognition',[
'Voting delegates who represent their chartered association at ILC are recognized with a **voting delegate ribbon**, **certificate** and **pin**.',
'Recognition reflects the importance of the role—delegates help guide the direction of the whole organization.',
"Recognition is also a reminder that the role carries accountability. Your chartered association trusted you to represent it well.",
"Recognition sessions also celebrate members’ achievements across HOSA, reminding delegates of the members they represent.",
chk('How are voting delegates recognized at ILC?',['With a ribbon, certificate and pin','With a trophy only','With cash prizes','They are not recognized'],'Guidelines list ribbon, certificate and pin.')]],
['Business session basics',[
'The **business session** is a formal meeting conducted with parliamentary procedure. Delegates may vote on business items and participate in electing the **Executive Council**. Delegates must understand motions, voting methods and how to obtain the floor.',
'Following orientation instructions and the presiding officer’s directions keeps the session orderly. The parliamentary procedure portion of the test (24%) prepares delegates for this meeting.',
"If you are unsure of a procedure during the session, you may ask a parliamentary inquiry through the chair or follow your delegation’s guidance from orientation.",
"Follow the presiding officer’s instructions on microphones, time limits and voting procedures. These rules keep a large assembly fair and efficient.",
"Keep the agenda and your notes together during the session so you can follow each item and know what is being voted on.",
ex('Preparing for the business session','Plan your preparation (example).',['Review the agenda in the Voting Delegate Packet','Review how to obtain the floor and make motions','Review voting methods and vote thresholds','Prepare notes on each candidate','Attend orientation and caucus'],'A prepared delegate participates confidently and correctly.')]],
['Communicating with your chartered association',[
'Before ILC, delegates may gather input from members and advisors about issues and candidates. After ILC, delegates can **report back** to their chartered association—sharing results, decisions and what they learned.',
'Clear communication strengthens trust and helps members understand how HOSA decisions are made.',
"A short written report or presentation to your chapter or state officers after ILC helps members feel connected to decisions made on their behalf.",
"Consider sharing a summary of new Executive Council officers and decisions in your chapter newsletter or social media, with your advisor’s approval.",
order('Delegate communication cycle',['Gather member and advisor input','Study materials','Participate at ILC','Vote thoughtfully','Report results back','Share lessons learned'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Delegate decisions',{a:{text:'You are selected as a voting delegate in April. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you review before ILC?',choices:[['The Voting Delegate Packet and Executive Council applicants’ resumes and videos','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing—just show up','b','Preparation is required.',false]]},b:{text:'At ILC, the Business Session conflicts with an event you want to watch. What do you do?',choices:[['Attend the Business Session—it is a required delegate duty','c','Correct.',true],['Skip it','c','Delegates must attend.',false]]},c:{text:'You fulfill every delegate responsibility.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Before ILC, delegates read the Voting Delegate Packet (sent to State Advisors 60 days before ILC) and review Executive Council applicants’ resumes and YouTube videos on the HOSA website.','At ILC, delegates attend Voting Delegate Orientation, the Meet the Candidates Breakfast, the Chartered Association Caucus (if desired), the Business Session and the Recognition Session; they are recognized with a ribbon, certificate and pin.','Delegates represent their whole chartered association with integrity, use parliamentary procedure in the business session and communicate before and after ILC.'],terms:[['Voting Delegate Packet','Materials delegates must read before ILC.'],['Caucus','A meeting of a chartered association’s delegates to discuss decisions.'],['Business Session','The formal ILC meeting where official business is conducted.']],mcqs:[q('When do State Advisors receive the Voting Delegate Packet?','60 days before ILC','The day of ILC','One year before','After ILC','Rule.'),q('What must delegates review besides the packet?','Executive Council applicants’ resumes and videos','Movie trailers','Sports scores','Other states’ test scores','Rule.'),q('Which session is optional (“if desired”)?','Chartered Association Caucus','Business Session','Voting Delegate Orientation','Recognition Session','Rule.'),q('Where are official votes conducted?','Business Session','Meet the Candidates Breakfast','Orientation','Recognition Session','Purpose.'),q('How are delegates recognized?','Ribbon, certificate and pin','Trophy','Scholarship','Medal','Rule.'),q('A delegate represents:','The entire chartered association','Only themself','Only their chapter','Only their friends','Role.'),q('Which is responsible delegate behavior?','Studying candidates before voting','Skipping sessions','Voting without reading materials','Ignoring orientation','Integrity.'),q('Where are candidates’ videos posted?','The HOSA website','A private chat','Television','They are not posted','Rule.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the responsibilities of a HOSA voting delegate before and during ILC.',rubric:[['Before ILC',['voting delegate packet','60 days','state advisor','resumes','videos','notes']],['At ILC',['orientation','meet the candidates','caucus','business session','recognition session']],['Representation',['chartered association','members','integrity','informed','fair']],['Recognition and communication',['ribbon','certificate','pin','report back','input']]],model:'After my chartered association notifies me that I have been selected as a voting delegate, I would prepare carefully before ILC. My State Advisor receives the Voting Delegate Packet from HOSA Headquarters 60 days before ILC, and I would read it completely, noting each item of business. I would also read every Executive Council applicant’s resume and watch their YouTube videos on the HOSA website, taking notes on their qualifications and goals. I would gather input from members and advisors in my chartered association about the issues and candidates. At ILC, I would attend all required sessions: Voting Delegate Orientation to learn procedures, the Meet the Candidates Breakfast to talk with candidates, the Chartered Association Caucus to discuss decisions with my fellow delegates, the Business Session, where official business is conducted with parliamentary procedure and the Executive Council is elected, and the Recognition Session. Throughout, I would remember that I represent my entire chartered association, not just my own preferences, and I would act with integrity, follow the presiding officer’s instructions and vote thoughtfully based on what best serves HOSA and our members. Delegates who fulfill these duties are recognized with a voting delegate ribbon, certificate and pin. After ILC, I would report the results and what I learned back to my chartered association.'},sources:[RULES,HOSA]};

export const OL3={title:'Evaluating candidates and elections',hook:'One of a voting delegate’s most important jobs is helping elect the student leaders who will serve HOSA for the year. Making a fair, informed decision means evaluating candidates’ qualifications and vision rather than popularity—and understanding how elections are conducted under parliamentary procedure.',objectives:['Evaluate candidates using objective criteria.','Use resumes, videos and meetings to gather information.','Explain nominations and election procedures under RONR.','Act ethically during elections.'],sections:[
['Gathering information',[
'Delegates evaluate **Executive Council applicants** using their **resumes**, **YouTube videos**, the **Meet the Candidates Breakfast** and any official materials in the Voting Delegate Packet. Each source shows something different: experience, communication, vision and interpersonal skills.',
'Take consistent notes on each candidate so you can compare them fairly later.',
"Read each resume for specific accomplishments, such as projects led or members served, rather than titles alone.",
"Prepare one or two respectful questions for the Meet the Candidates Breakfast. Thoughtful questions can reveal how candidates think and communicate.",
"If materials are limited for a candidate, focus on the evidence available rather than filling gaps with assumptions.",
match('Information source and what it shows',[['Resume','Experience and accomplishments'],['Video','Communication and vision'],['Meet the Candidates Breakfast','Interpersonal skills and responses'],['Voting Delegate Packet','Official procedures and information'],['Caucus discussion','Your delegation’s perspectives'],['Your notes','Fair comparison']])]],
['Using objective criteria',[
'Create a simple **evaluation rubric** with criteria such as **leadership experience**, **knowledge of HOSA**, **communication skills**, **vision and goals**, **professionalism** and **commitment to serving members**. Rate each candidate on the same scale.',
'Objective criteria reduce bias and help you explain your decisions to your chartered association.',
"Weight criteria by importance if your delegation agrees. For example, vision and commitment to members might count more than presentation style.",
"Revisit your ratings after the breakfast. New information may change your evaluation, and updating ratings based on evidence is part of fair judgment.",
"Discuss and agree on criteria with your delegation before reviewing candidates, so everyone evaluates using the same standards.",
ex('A candidate rubric','Rate two candidates from 1 to 5 on four criteria (ratings invented for practice).',['Candidate A: leadership 5, HOSA knowledge 4, communication 4, vision 5 = 18','Candidate B: leadership 4, HOSA knowledge 5, communication 3, vision 3 = 15','Discuss ratings at the caucus','Vote based on the best overall fit'],'Candidate A rates higher overall (ratings invented for practice).')]],
['Avoiding bias',[
'Common biases include voting for someone because they are **from your region or state**, a **friend**, **popular** or **memorable for a gimmick** rather than qualified. Others include judging by appearance or being swayed by the last candidate you met.',
'Recognize bias by asking: “Would I rate this candidate the same if I did not know them?” Base decisions on evidence from your notes.',
"Write down a reason for each rating. Recording evidence makes it easier to stay objective and explain your choice later.",
"Popularity on social media is not the same as qualification. Focus on what candidates have accomplished and what they plan to do for members.",
sort('Fair or biased reason to vote?',['Fair reason','Biased reason'],[['Strong record of chapter leadership',0],['Candidate is my best friend',1],['Clear, realistic goals for members',0],['Candidate gave out the best giveaways',1],['Knowledge of HOSA bylaws and programs',0],['Candidate is from my state, regardless of qualifications',1]])]],
['Nominations and elections under RONR',[
'Under Robert’s Rules, elections usually begin with **nominations**, which may come from a **nominating committee** and/or from the floor (nominations from the floor need no second). After nominations close, members vote—often by **ballot** for officer elections.',
'Unless the bylaws provide otherwise, a candidate must receive a **majority of votes cast** to be elected; a plurality is not enough. If no one has a majority, balloting continues. HOSA’s specific election procedures appear in its bylaws and the Voting Delegate Packet.',
"Under RONR, a motion to close nominations requires a two-thirds vote, though nominations are often closed by general consent when no one objects.",
"Pay attention to instructions about ballots during orientation. Correctly marked ballots ensure your vote is counted as intended.",
"Tellers count ballots and report results to the chair, who then declares the outcome. Accurate counting protects the fairness of the election.",
order('Typical election procedure (RONR)',['Nominations are made','Nominations are closed','Members vote, often by ballot','Tellers count votes','Results are reported','Chair declares who is elected'])]],
['Ethics during elections',[
'Delegates and candidates should follow HOSA’s rules and act with integrity: honest communication, respect for all candidates, no pressure or deals to trade votes and respect for the secrecy of ballots.',
'If you see behavior that may violate rules, report it to your State Advisor or appropriate officials rather than handling it yourself.',
"Treat every candidate with courtesy, including those you do not support. Respectful behavior reflects well on you and your chartered association.",
"Campaign rules may limit how candidates and supporters promote themselves. Delegates should be aware of these rules and avoid encouraging violations.",
chk('Another delegate offers to vote for your preferred candidate if you vote for theirs. What do you do?',['Decline and vote based on your evaluation','Agree to the trade','Spread rumors about candidates','Skip voting'],'Vote with integrity.')]],
['Discussing candidates at the caucus',[
'At the **Chartered Association Caucus**, delegates can share notes, discuss strengths and concerns and consider members’ input. Listen respectfully, speak about qualifications rather than personalities and make decisions thoughtfully.',
'Remember that each delegate votes as a representative, with an informed and independent judgment.',
"If your delegation disagrees, focus on shared criteria and evidence. A respectful discussion often leads to better-informed decisions.",
"After discussion, each delegate still casts an individual vote. The caucus informs decisions, but it does not replace each delegate’s responsibility.",
order('A productive caucus',['Review each candidate’s qualifications','Share notes and ratings','Discuss strengths and concerns respectfully','Consider member and advisor input','Make informed decisions','Prepare for the Business Session'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Election choices',{a:{text:'A candidate’s video is entertaining but says little about goals. Another is less flashy but presents a clear plan. (Scenario invented for practice.) How do you evaluate them?',choices:[['Use your rubric—rate vision and goals on evidence, not entertainment','b','Correct.',true],['Choose the funniest video','b','Use objective criteria.',false]]},b:{text:'No candidate receives a majority of votes cast. Under RONR, what happens unless bylaws say otherwise?',choices:[['Balloting continues until someone receives a majority','c','Correct.',true],['The candidate with the most votes wins automatically','c','A plurality does not elect under RONR.',false]]},c:{text:'You make a fair, informed decision.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Evaluate Executive Council applicants using resumes, YouTube videos, the Meet the Candidates Breakfast and packet materials, with consistent notes.','Use objective criteria (leadership, HOSA knowledge, communication, vision, professionalism, commitment) and recognize biases such as friendship, region, popularity and gimmicks.','Under RONR, nominations (floor nominations need no second) precede voting, often by ballot; a majority of votes cast elects unless bylaws say otherwise; act ethically and discuss qualifications respectfully at the caucus.'],terms:[['Nomination','Proposing a person as a candidate for office.'],['Ballot','A secret written vote.'],['Plurality','The most votes, but not necessarily a majority.']],mcqs:[q('Which source shows a candidate’s communication and vision?','Their YouTube video','Their birthplace','Their shoe size','A rumor','Source.'),q('Which is a fair reason to vote for a candidate?','A strong record of leadership','They are your friend','They gave the best giveaways','They are from your state only','Fairness.'),q('Under RONR, what is needed to be elected unless bylaws say otherwise?','A majority of votes cast','A plurality','Unanimous vote','Two-thirds','Rule.'),q('Do nominations from the floor need a second?','No','Yes','Two seconds','Only for president','Rule.'),q('If no one gets a majority under RONR, what happens?','Balloting continues','The highest vote-getter wins','The election is canceled','The chair decides','Rule.'),q('A vote trade offer should be:','Declined','Accepted','Encouraged','Kept secret and accepted','Ethics.'),q('A rubric helps delegates:','Compare candidates fairly','Avoid reading materials','Vote randomly','Skip the caucus','Objectivity.'),q('Officer elections are often conducted by:','Ballot','Shouting','Coin toss','Text message','Method.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a voting delegate should evaluate Executive Council candidates and how elections work under parliamentary procedure.',rubric:[['Information',['resumes','videos','breakfast','packet','notes']],['Objective evaluation',['criteria','rubric','leadership','vision','communication','bias']],['Election procedure',['nominations','ballot','majority','votes cast','balloting continues','bylaws']],['Ethics',['integrity','no trades','respect','report','caucus']]],model:'To evaluate Executive Council candidates, I would gather information from several sources: each applicant’s resume for leadership experience and accomplishments, their YouTube video for communication and vision, the Meet the Candidates Breakfast for interpersonal skills and the Voting Delegate Packet for official procedures. I would take consistent notes and use a simple rubric rating each candidate from 1 to 5 on leadership experience, knowledge of HOSA, communication, vision and goals, professionalism and commitment to members. This would help me avoid biases such as favoring a friend, a candidate from my own region, the most popular person or someone with flashy giveaways, and I would ask myself whether I would rate each candidate the same if I did not know them. At the Chartered Association Caucus, I would share my notes respectfully, focus on qualifications rather than personalities and consider members’ and advisors’ input while keeping my own informed judgment. Under Robert’s Rules, elections begin with nominations—from a nominating committee or the floor, where they need no second—followed by voting, often by ballot, with tellers counting and reporting results. Unless the bylaws say otherwise, a candidate needs a majority of votes cast, not just a plurality, and balloting continues until someone has a majority; HOSA’s specific procedures are in its bylaws and the packet. I would act with integrity, refuse any vote trades, respect ballot secrecy and report concerns to my State Advisor.'},sources:[RULES,RONR]};

export const OL4={title:'Parliamentary procedure: meetings and voting',hook:'Parliamentary procedure is 24% of the test and the language of the business session. Delegates must know how meetings are run, how to obtain the floor, how motions are handled and exactly how votes are taken and counted—down to saying “aye” and “no.” This lesson covers the foundations from Robert’s Rules of Order Newly Revised In Brief.',objectives:['Explain the principles and structure of a meeting.','Obtain the floor and handle a main motion correctly.','Use correct voting language and methods.','Apply majority and two-thirds thresholds.'],sections:[
['Principles and the meeting',[
'Parliamentary procedure protects **majority rule** while respecting **minority rights**, considers **one item at a time** and ensures **full and free discussion**. A **quorum**—the minimum number needed to conduct business—is a **majority of the members** unless the bylaws say otherwise.',
'A standard **order of business** includes call to order, minutes, reports of officers and committees, unfinished business, new business and adjournment. HOSA’s business session follows an agenda provided to delegates.',
"Minutes record what was done, not what was said, including motions, their makers and results. Delegates may approve minutes as part of business.",
"The agenda for the business session is usually included in the Voting Delegate Packet, so study it before arriving.",
order('Standard order of business',['Call to order','Reading and approval of minutes','Reports of officers','Reports of committees','Unfinished business','New business','Adjournment'])]],
['Obtaining the floor',[
'To speak, a delegate **obtains the floor**: goes to a microphone or rises, addresses the chair (“Madam President” or “Mr. President,” or “Madam/Mr. Chair”) and waits to be **recognized**. The chair refers to themself as “**the chair**.”',
'In large assemblies such as the business session, delegates may need to state their name and chartered association when recognized. Follow orientation instructions for the specific procedure used.',
"Practice the phrase you will use when recognized, such as stating your name and chartered association, so you can speak confidently.",
"Speakers in large assemblies are often limited in time. Plan the key point you want to make so you can say it clearly within the limit.",
chk('How does a delegate begin to speak in the business session?',['Address the chair and wait to be recognized','Start talking from their seat','Shout to get attention','Text the chair'],'Obtain the floor.')]],
['Handling a main motion',[
'A main motion follows six steps: a member **makes the motion** (“I move that…”); another **seconds** it; the chair **states the question**; members **debate**; the chair **puts the question** to a vote; the chair **announces the result**.',
'A main motion needs a **second**, is **debatable** and **amendable** and usually needs a **majority vote**. Use “**I move**,” not “I make a motion.”',
"A main motion can be made only when no other business is pending. If something else is being discussed, wait until it is resolved.",
"Before the chair states the question, the maker may withdraw or modify a motion freely; afterward, the motion belongs to the assembly.",
order('Six steps of a main motion',['Member makes the motion','Another member seconds','Chair states the question','Members debate','Chair puts the question to a vote','Chair announces the result'])]],
['Voting language and methods',[
'In a voice vote, the chair says, “**Those in favor, say aye.**” then “**Those opposed, say no.**” A member **not in favor** votes by saying “**no**”—the sample test item’s answer. Other methods include **rising vote**, **show of hands**, **ballot**, **roll call** and **general consent** (“If there is no objection…”).',
'The chair always takes the negative vote and announces the full result: “The ayes have it, and the motion is adopted.”',
"If you doubt the result of a voice vote, any member may call for a “division” to require a rising or counted vote.",
"General consent saves time on routine items. The chair asks if there is any objection; if none is heard, the action is approved.",
match('Voting method and use',[['Voice vote','Most majority votes'],['Rising vote','Two-thirds votes or doubtful results'],['Ballot','Elections and secret votes'],['Show of hands','Small groups'],['General consent','Routine, uncontested matters'],['Division of the assembly','Member demands a counted or rising vote']])]],
['Vote thresholds',[
'A **majority** is **more than half of the votes cast**, excluding abstentions and blanks. A **two-thirds vote** means at least twice as many yes votes as no votes. Two-thirds is required for motions that limit members’ rights, such as **closing debate (previous question)**, **limiting or extending debate** and **suspending the rules**.',
'A **tie** vote means a motion requiring a majority is **lost**.',
"Abstaining means not voting. Abstentions do not count as votes cast, so they do not affect a majority based on votes cast.",
"Understanding thresholds helps you predict outcomes. If your delegation supports closing debate, you know you need at least twice as many yes votes as no votes.",
ex('Calculating thresholds','90 delegates vote on closing debate: 58 yes, 32 no (invented for practice). Does it pass?',['Closing debate requires two-thirds','Twice the no votes: 2 × 32 = 64','58 is less than 64','The motion fails; debate continues'],'It fails—58 to 32 is a majority but not two-thirds (numbers invented for practice).')]],
['Decorum in a large assembly',[
'In a large business session, **decorum** keeps the meeting orderly: speak only when recognized, address remarks to the chair, stay on topic, be concise, respect time limits and speak about issues rather than individuals.',
'Delegates represent their chartered associations, so professional behavior reflects on their state and on HOSA.',
"Keep comments short in large assemblies. Clear, two- or three-sentence remarks are more effective and respect others’ time.",
"Listen carefully to other speakers. Responding to points already made, rather than repeating them, contributes more to debate.",
sort('Proper decorum?',['Proper','Improper'],[['Waiting to be recognized at the microphone',0],['Calling out from the floor',1],['Addressing remarks through the chair',0],['Criticizing a candidate personally',1],['Keeping comments brief and on topic',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Business session drill',{a:{text:'The chair says, “Those opposed, say…” What word completes the call? (Practice item.)',choices:[['“No”','b','Correct.',true],['“Nay”','b','RONR uses aye and no.',false]]},b:{text:'A motion to suspend the rules receives 60 yes and 40 no. Does it pass?',choices:[['No—60 is less than two-thirds (twice 40 is 80)','c','Correct.',true],['Yes—it has a majority','c','Suspending the rules needs two-thirds.',false]]},c:{text:'You are ready for business session votes.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Parliamentary procedure protects majority rule and minority rights, one item at a time; a quorum is a majority of members unless bylaws say otherwise; follow the order of business and agenda.','Obtain the floor by addressing the chair and being recognized; a main motion is made (“I move”), seconded, stated, debated, put to vote and announced; it needs a second and a majority.','Voice votes use “aye” and “no”; other methods include rising, hands, ballot, roll call and general consent; majority is more than half of votes cast; two-thirds is needed to close or limit debate and suspend the rules; ties lose.'],terms:[['Quorum','Minimum number of members needed to conduct business.'],['Obtaining the floor','Being recognized by the chair to speak.'],['Two-thirds vote','At least twice as many yes votes as no votes.']],mcqs:[q('How does a member not in favor vote in a voice vote?','“No”','“Nay”','“Negative”','By leaving','Sample item.'),q('What is a quorum unless bylaws say otherwise?','A majority of the members','All members','Two members','One-third','Default.'),q('How should a member make a motion?','“I move that…”','“I make a motion…”','“I want…”','“Let’s…”','Wording.'),q('A majority means:','More than half of votes cast','Half of members present','The most votes','Two-thirds','Definition.'),q('Which requires a two-thirds vote?','Suspending the rules','A main motion','Adjourning','Referring to committee','Threshold.'),q('A tie vote on a main motion means it is:','Lost','Adopted','Tabled','Referred','Rule.'),q('The chair refers to themself as:','“The chair”','“I”','“Me”','“Your boss”','Convention.'),q('Which vote method is used for elections?','Ballot','Voice only','Shouting','None','Method.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a business session is conducted, how motions are handled and how votes are taken and counted.',rubric:[['Meeting basics',['majority rule','minority','quorum','order of business','agenda']],['Floor and motions',['address the chair','recognized','i move','second','debate','announce']],['Voting',['aye','no','voice','rising','ballot','general consent']],['Thresholds',['majority','votes cast','two-thirds','previous question','suspend','tie']]],model:'A business session follows parliamentary procedure, which protects majority rule and minority rights, takes up one item at a time and allows full discussion, with a quorum—a majority of members unless the bylaws say otherwise—required to conduct business. The meeting follows an order of business: call to order, minutes, officer and committee reports, unfinished business, new business and adjournment, or the agenda provided to delegates. To speak, a delegate goes to a microphone, addresses the chair as “Madam President” or “Mr. President” and waits to be recognized, often stating their name and chartered association. A main motion follows six steps: a delegate says, “I move that…,” another seconds, the chair states the question, delegates debate, the chair puts the question to a vote and announces the result; it needs a second, is debatable and amendable and usually requires a majority. In a voice vote, the chair says, “Those in favor, say aye. Those opposed, say no,” so a delegate against the motion says “no.” Other methods include rising votes, show of hands, ballots for elections, roll call and general consent for routine matters, and the chair always takes the negative vote. A majority is more than half of the votes cast, excluding abstentions, while two-thirds—required for closing or limiting debate and suspending the rules—means at least twice as many yes as no votes. A tie means the motion is lost. Delegates maintain decorum by speaking only when recognized, staying on topic and addressing the chair.'},sources:[RULES,RONR]};

export const OL5={title:'Parliamentary procedure: motions in the business session',hook:'Business sessions move quickly, and delegates must recognize which motions are in order and what they do. The sample test question about postpone indefinitely shows that motion purposes are tested directly. This lesson covers the subsidiary, privileged and incidental motions delegates are most likely to see.',objectives:['Explain the purposes of common subsidiary motions.','Describe privileged and incidental motions.','Apply the order of precedence.','Use amendments correctly.'],sections:[
['Subsidiary motions',[
'Subsidiary motions help the assembly handle a main motion. **Postpone indefinitely** disposes of the main motion without a direct vote—the sample item describes it as a way **to dispose of an embarrassing main motion**. **Amend** changes the wording. **Commit or refer** sends it to a committee. **Postpone to a certain time** delays it to a specific time. **Limit or extend debate** changes debate rules (two-thirds). **Previous question** closes debate (two-thirds). **Lay on the table** sets it aside temporarily for something urgent.',
'Each has a distinct purpose—choose the motion that matches what you want to accomplish.',
"Lay on the table is not debatable or amendable and needs a majority. It should not be used to kill a motion; postpone indefinitely is the proper motion for that.",
"Postpone indefinitely is debatable and opens the main question to debate, but it is not amendable. It needs a second and a majority vote.",
match('Motion and purpose',[['Postpone indefinitely','Dispose of the main motion without a direct vote'],['Amend','Change the wording'],['Commit or refer','Send to a committee'],['Postpone to a certain time','Delay to a specific time'],['Previous question','Close debate (two-thirds)'],['Lay on the table','Set aside temporarily for urgent business']])]],
['Amendments',[
'Amendments may **insert or add**, **strike out** or **strike out and insert** words, or **substitute** a whole paragraph or motion. A **primary amendment** amends the main motion; a **secondary amendment** amends the primary amendment; **third-degree amendments are not allowed**. Amendments must be **germane** (related to the subject).',
'Amendments are voted on from the most recent to the main motion: secondary, then primary (as amended), then the main motion (as amended). Sample test distractors may mention making motions “more germane,” but that is not the purpose of postpone indefinitely.',
"A substitute replaces an entire paragraph or motion and is treated as a form of strike out and insert.",
"Practice identifying which motion fits a goal: kill a motion, delay it, study it, change it or set it aside. Matching goals to motions is a common test skill.",
order('Voting on amendments',['Secondary amendment','Primary amendment as amended','Main motion as amended'])]],
['Privileged motions',[
'Privileged motions address urgent matters unrelated to pending business and rank highest: **fix the time to which to adjourn**, **adjourn**, **recess**, **raise a question of privilege** (for example, the hall is too noisy) and **call for the orders of the day** (demanding the agenda be followed).',
'Recess and adjourn need a second, are not debatable and need a majority; questions of privilege and calls for the orders of the day need no second.',
"Recess and adjourn are privileged only when other business is pending; otherwise they may be treated as main motions.",
"A question of privilege might concern noise, temperature or the ability to hear the speaker—issues that affect delegates’ ability to participate.",
sort('Privileged or subsidiary motion?',['Privileged','Subsidiary'],[['Recess',0],['Amend',1],['Raise a question of privilege',0],['Refer to a committee',1],['Call for the orders of the day',0],['Postpone indefinitely',1]])]],
['Incidental motions',[
'Incidental motions deal with procedure. **Point of order** claims a rule is being broken; the chair rules on it (no second). **Appeal** asks the assembly to overrule the chair (second; a tie sustains the chair). **Parliamentary inquiry** asks a procedural question. **Request for information** asks a factual question. **Division of the assembly** demands a rising or counted vote. **Suspend the rules** requires two-thirds and cannot suspend the bylaws.',
'Delegates use these to keep the session fair and clear.',
"A point of order must be raised promptly when the breach occurs. The chair rules, and members may appeal if they disagree.",
"A parliamentary inquiry is a helpful tool for new delegates. It lets you ask the chair how to accomplish something without breaking rules.",
match('Incidental motion and use',[['Point of order','A rule is being broken'],['Appeal','Ask the assembly to overrule the chair'],['Parliamentary inquiry','Ask about procedure'],['Request for information','Ask a factual question'],['Division of the assembly','Demand a counted vote'],['Suspend the rules','Set aside a rule of order (two-thirds)']])]],
['Order of precedence',[
'Motions rank in order. From highest to lowest: fix the time to which to adjourn, adjourn, recess, question of privilege, orders of the day, lay on the table, previous question, limit or extend debate, postpone to a certain time, commit, amend, postpone indefinitely and the main motion. While a motion is pending, only higher-ranking motions may be made. Pending motions are voted on in **reverse order** of making.',
'Incidental motions arise as needed and do not have a fixed rank.',
"When you are unsure whether a motion is in order, compare its rank with the pending motion. Only higher-ranking motions can be made.",
order('Precedence (highest to lowest)',['Fix the time to which to adjourn','Adjourn','Recess','Question of privilege','Orders of the day','Lay on the table','Previous question','Limit or extend debate','Postpone to a certain time','Commit or refer','Amend','Postpone indefinitely','Main motion'])]],
['Bringing motions back',[
'**Reconsider** brings back a motion decided the same day; only a member who voted on the **prevailing side** may move it (majority vote). **Rescind** or **amend something previously adopted** changes earlier decisions (two-thirds, or a majority with previous notice, or a majority of the entire membership). **Take from the table** resumes a tabled motion (majority).',
'Knowing these lets delegates understand how an assembly can correct or revisit decisions.',
"Previous notice for rescinding can be given at the prior meeting or in the call of the meeting, allowing a majority vote instead of two-thirds.",
chk('Who may move to reconsider a vote?',['A member who voted on the prevailing side','Any member at any time','Only the chair','Only absent members'],'Prevailing-side rule.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Motion purposes',{a:{text:'A delegate wants to dispose of an embarrassing main motion without a direct vote on it. (Practice item.) Which motion fits?',choices:[['Postpone indefinitely','b','Correct—matches the sample item.',true],['Postpone to a certain time','b','That delays the motion, it does not dispose of it.',false]]},b:{text:'A main motion and an amendment are pending. A delegate moves to refer the motion to a committee. Is it in order?',choices:[['Yes—commit ranks above amend','c','Correct.',true],['No','c','Higher-ranking motions are in order.',false]]},c:{text:'You recognize motions quickly and correctly.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Subsidiary motions: postpone indefinitely (dispose of a main motion, such as an embarrassing one), amend, commit, postpone to a certain time, limit or extend debate and previous question (two-thirds) and lay on the table (temporary).','Amendments insert, strike, strike and insert or substitute; primary and secondary only, germane, voted secondary to primary to main; privileged motions (fix time to adjourn, adjourn, recess, question of privilege, orders of the day) outrank subsidiary motions.','Incidental motions include point of order, appeal, parliamentary inquiry, request for information, division and suspend the rules; motions follow precedence and are voted in reverse order; reconsider, rescind and take from the table bring motions back.'],terms:[['Subsidiary motion','A motion that helps handle a pending main motion.'],['Privileged motion','An urgent motion unrelated to pending business.'],['Germane','Closely related to the subject of the motion.']],mcqs:[q('Postpone indefinitely is used to:','Dispose of an embarrassing main motion','Postpone to the next meeting','Make a motion more germane','Recess','Sample item.'),q('Which motion sends a motion to a committee?','Commit or refer','Recess','Adjourn','Amend','Purpose.'),q('Are third-degree amendments allowed?','No','Yes','Only for bylaws','Only by the chair','Rule.'),q('Which is a privileged motion?','Recess','Amend','Commit','Postpone indefinitely','Class.'),q('Which ranks highest?','Fix the time to which to adjourn','Main motion','Amend','Commit','Precedence.'),q('A point of order:','Claims a rule is being broken','Closes debate','Elects officers','Adjourns the meeting','Purpose.'),q('A tie vote on an appeal:','Sustains the chair','Overturns the chair','Ends the meeting','Requires a revote','Rule.'),q('Pending motions are voted on in:','Reverse order of making','The order made','Alphabetical order','Random order','Rule.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the purposes of key subsidiary, privileged and incidental motions and how the order of precedence works in a business session.',rubric:[['Subsidiary',['postpone indefinitely','embarrassing','amend','commit','previous question','lay on the table']],['Amendments',['primary','secondary','third','germane','reverse order']],['Privileged and incidental',['recess','adjourn','question of privilege','point of order','appeal','division']],['Precedence and bring-back',['precedence','higher','reverse','reconsider','prevailing side','rescind']]],model:'Subsidiary motions help an assembly handle a main motion: postpone indefinitely disposes of it without a direct vote, which is why the sample test item describes it as a way to dispose of an embarrassing main motion; amend changes its wording; commit or refer sends it to a committee; postpone to a certain time delays it; limit or extend debate and the previous question require two-thirds because they restrict debate; and lay on the table sets it aside temporarily for urgent business. Amendments may insert, strike out, strike out and insert or substitute, and only primary and secondary amendments are allowed, both germane, voted from the secondary to the primary to the main motion as amended. Privileged motions deal with urgent matters and rank highest: fix the time to which to adjourn, adjourn, recess, raise a question of privilege and call for the orders of the day. Incidental motions keep procedure fair: a point of order claims a rule is broken and the chair rules, an appeal lets the assembly overrule the chair with a tie sustaining the chair, parliamentary inquiries and requests for information ask questions, division of the assembly demands a counted vote and suspending the rules requires two-thirds but cannot suspend the bylaws. Under the order of precedence, only higher-ranking motions may be made while others are pending, and pending motions are voted on in reverse order. Finally, reconsider, rescind and take from the table allow decisions to be revisited.'},sources:[RULES,RONR,NAP]};
